{"context":"Three problems:\n\n1. **The init skeleton is obsolete for new repositories.** `eka init` scaffolds the legacy docs-in-repo skeleton (docs/ tree, README, workflow docs). The current EKA line (standard + core) consistently uses the EKA WORKSPACE as the knowledge location; docs-in-repo is ONLY backward compatibility. The skeleton scaffold is therefore obsolete for new repositories.\n2. **No explicit identity at init.** `eka init` has no way to set the identity explicitly: eka.yaml project == namespace == a single basename-derived value, and the wizard couples them. Users cannot declare a project id distinct from the unit namespace, and there are no inline flags (machine/CI).\n3. **The namespace duality bug (verified end-to-end).** eka.yaml/repos.namespace (the declared identity) and the CONTENT namespace (frontmatter of the docs/JSON artifacts, which IS the unit identity — P3: identity lives in the file body) are never reconciled. `eka sync --from-docs` compiles units with the content namespace while repos.namespace stays the eka.yaml value → `eka get \u003cns\u003e/...` and the snapshot label use different namespaces → consume-by-namespace breaks even though `eka view` (project union) shows everything. The committed reference project reproduces this (eka.yaml namespace `eka-ref-impl` vs content `feather`).","decision":"### Decision 1 — `eka init` becomes identity-only\n\n- `eka init` no longer scaffolds the docs-in-repo skeleton: no docs/ tree, no README generation, no skeleton file copies. The generated repository contains ONLY `eka.yaml` (+ optional git init). Knowledge lives in the EKA workspace (sync/publish); docs-in-repo is a legacy authoring adapter, never generated.\n- The embedded skeleton (skeletonembed.go + bootstrap skeleton readers) is removed from the build; the `skeleton/` directory remains in the repository as reference documentation (it is linked from conformance docs, e.g. `skeleton/docs/exchange/validation.md` is the rules source).\n- The wizard shrinks: project id, namespace, git-init questions only (display name / description / README questions die with the skeleton). Post-generation validation still runs (with the no-docs skip semantics of ADR-018).\n- Existing-dir adoption and existing-EKA adoption (ADR-018) are unchanged in spirit: init on an existing dir/repo writes only `eka.yaml` (+ optional git), never docs.\n\n### Decision 2 — explicit identity options in `eka init`\n\n- Inline flags: `eka init [name] --project \u003cid\u003e --namespace \u003cns\u003e` (machine/CI). Defaults stay deterministic: project = namespace = sanitized directory basename.\n- Interactive wizard: separate prompts for the project id and the namespace (decoupled — eka.yaml `project` no longer forced to equal `namespace`), with defaults.\n- eka.yaml semantics: `project` = workspace project grouping; `name` = directory basename; `namespace` = unit identity prefix (platform scope). `project` and `namespace` are independent.\n\n### Decision 3 — content namespace reconciliation (identity follows content)\n\n- The CONTENT namespace is authoritative for unit identity (P3: identity lives in the file body, not in external files). eka.yaml/repos.namespace must equal the content namespace.\n- `eka sync` and `eka project register` DETECT the mismatch: when the repository's content (docs tree artifacts and/or the units seeded from the snapshot) resolves to exactly ONE distinct namespace and it differs from the declared namespace, the command refuses deterministically with a hint.\n- OVERRIDE mechanism (explicit, sanctioned identity-change path — amends ADR-017 D5 immutability): `--override` flag (inline/machine) or an interactive confirmation (TTY, arrow-selected option) aligns the IDENTITY to the content: eka.yaml `namespace` is rewritten and repos.namespace is updated. After alignment the identity freezes again.\n- Content spanning MULTIPLE distinct namespaces: refusal without override (a repository is one platform; consolidate content first).\n- First sync of a fresh repository (no content yet) is unaffected.\n\n### Decision 4 — reference project correction\n\n- `reference/project/eka.yaml` namespace corrected: `eka-ref-impl` → `feather` (matches its content — the Feather demo). The ADR-017 spec pin (`reference/spec-repository-identity.md` §6 Step 6) is amended. The snapshot is regenerated (header namespace `feather`, label `rsf-repo-feather-2` — expected byte-identical to the pre-ADR-017 snapshot, digest `66a1ac03…` — determinism proof; verify during implementation).\n- `eka get feather/...` must work; `eka get eka-ref-impl/...` must NOT (that namespace belongs to the ADRs in `reference/decisions`, which are outside this repository's docs tree).","consequences":"- **`eka init` is identity-only**: a fresh repository carries only `eka.yaml` (+ optional git init); knowledge enters via the EKA workspace (sync/publish). The embedded skeleton leaves the build; `skeleton/` stays in the repository as reference documentation.\n- **Explicit identity**: CI/machine init via `--project`/`--namespace` flags; the wizard decouples project id from namespace with deterministic defaults (project = namespace = sanitized directory basename).\n- **Refusal/override flow** (byte-pinned strings in the implementation spec §6A.3; both commands exit 2):\n  1. the ADR-018 context gate passes, then detection runs **before any store mutation**;\n  2. content resolves to exactly ONE distinct namespace differing from the declared one → deterministic refusal with the override hint (single-ns), exit 2, **no changes written**;\n  3. content spans MULTIPLE distinct namespaces → refusal without override (a repository is one platform — consolidate the content), exit 2;\n  4. TTY: arrow-selected confirmation; abort → the same refusal string, exit 2;\n  5. override: eka.yaml `namespace` rewritten to the content namespace (`project`/`name` untouched), repos.namespace updated — then the identity freezes again.\n- **`--override` is the only identity change after the first sync** (amends ADR-017 D5).\n- **Reference project correction**: eka.yaml namespace `feather`; snapshot regenerated (header `feather`, label `rsf-repo-feather-2`, digest `66a1ac03…` expected — byte-identical to the pre-ADR-017 snapshot: the determinism proof); `eka get feather/...` works, `eka get eka-ref-impl/...` does not.\n- **Breaking**: init no longer produces the docs skeleton or README; workflows consuming them move to the workspace; the skeleton embed is gone from the binary.\n- Implementation checklist and file-by-file details: `reference/spec-repository-context.md` §6A.\n\n### Compatibility \u0026 migration\n\n- **Existing stores**: the first sync after upgrade on a repository whose content namespace differs from the declared one hits the deterministic refusal; the fix is the sanctioned `--override` path (or the TTY confirmation) — the only identity change after the first sync.\n- **Legacy docs repositories** (docs/ tree, no eka.yaml): adopt via `eka init` (writes only `eka.yaml` — never the skeleton), then the first sync reconciles: content namespace matches the declared one → registers cleanly; mismatch → refusal + override hint.\n- **Fresh repositories**: init is identity-only; the first sync with no content yet is unaffected by detection.\n- Implementation checklist and file-by-file details: `reference/spec-repository-context.md` §6A.","alternativesConsidered":"- **Keep skeleton generation** — rejected: docs-in-repo is legacy and the EKA workspace is canonical; scaffolding a legacy adapter into every new repository perpetuates the wrong mental model (ADR-018 already made the docs tree optional and validation skips cleanly without it).\n- **Content-follows-identity rewrite at compile** — rejected: rewriting unit identities and references at compile time violates P3 (identity lives in the file body) and detaches history; content is the authority, never rewritten.\n- **Silent auto-align without confirmation** — rejected: identity changes must be explicit (ADR-017 D5); a silent namespace rewrite would void the frozen-identity guarantee.\n- **Multi-ns pick-most-common** — rejected: a repository is one platform; a silent majority vote hides content that belongs elsewhere and breaks consume-by-namespace for the minority.\n- **Snapshot header follows eka.yaml** — rejected: the snapshot header must equal its unit namespaces (round-trip); a header that disagrees with its units reproduces the duality bug inside the snapshot."}